186537494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537856 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537856 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537856 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537856 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537856 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186537663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537856 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cc272370-ecec-5e06-8147-00a26161afa7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537908 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537908 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537908 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537908 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=4,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y\\sqrt{x}\\,i+(z-x)\\,j+{y}^{3}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537908 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46a23183-289b-59ee-a9e5-bbe0aa79406a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537908 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\sqrt{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\sqrt{x}]_{x=1}^{x=4}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537916 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537916 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537916 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537916 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=\\pi ,$ $z=2,$ $z=4.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={z}^{3}\\,i+2xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537916 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56f4d79a-05af-516b-82f5-f4f41e31d95b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537916 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+0 \\\\  & =-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{2}^{4}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=2}^{z=4} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)\\cdot (\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538010 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186538031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538010 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3dce55ae-d084-5337-aee1-d8317edb813b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538010 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186537920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538017 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186538010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538017 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4ab52d9d-1386-5abc-93b3-5a289a8a33fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538017 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186537972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538024 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186538017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538024 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "56f15a00-caa2-5ac5-aa05-f8b56b93bcf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538024 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186134139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538031 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186538036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538031 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79949f49-23cd-5a73-93a6-eaeb03dd99b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538031 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186537916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538036 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "94c36b82-7cd0-559d-9b66-17b2de79c924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538036 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186537908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186556453 #edb/ref 1080 "y+3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186556453 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186556453 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bc77a23-0eee-55f5-af88-ab3ad6a354e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186556458 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}