-531d-bc45-e785a72a6f50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557238 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557238 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557238 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=3,$ $y=-1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=4.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y{e}^{x}\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+3z\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557238 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d423292-2025-559a-9094-6ebc6fb41834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557238 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}+0+3 \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(y{e}^{x}+3)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,y{e}^{x}+3\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557290 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557290 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557290 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557290 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x\\sqrt{y}\\,i-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j+xy\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557290 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a809785e-b48f-5cb0-92f3-ff24733e01ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557290 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}+0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557319 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557319 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557319 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557319 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=2,$ and the surfaces $x=y-z$ and $x=1+{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=i+x{z}^{3}\\,j+xz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557319 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e44dc84b-de4c-5508-b285-9f3d93ffdc55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557319 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz) \\\\  & =0+0+x \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,y-z\\le x\\le 1+{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
